    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`, and all 6 tests pass: the 3 existing ones and 3 new ones.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. It calls `session.call_tool(name, arguments)`.\n2. If the result is marked as an error (`is_error`), it raises `ToolCallFailed(name, message)`. The message is the result's text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`.\n3. Otherwise, if the result has structured output (`structured_content is not None`), it returns that value unchanged.\n4. Otherwise it returns the text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`.\n\nA small helper, `_text`, does the text joining for both the error and the plain-text cases. I used the field names from the installed mcp 2.2.0 (`is_error`, `structured_content`) and imported the types from `mcp.types`. No dependencies were added.\n\n**Tests:** `tests/test_calls.py` runs `run_tool` against an in-memory `MCPServer`, covering structured output, text-only output, and a failing tool.\n\n**Behaviour to be aware of:**\n- **Server text is passed on unchanged.** The server adds its own prefix to the message, so a failure comes through as `Error executing tool deploy: no such version 9.9`.\n- **Crashes lose their details.** The server only sends the tool's message when the tool raises `ToolError`. If it raises anything else, the client only sees `Error executing tool <name>`.\n- **Other errors don't become `ToolCallFailed`.** If the server rejects the request itself (for example, an unknown tool name reported as an MCP error) or the client's output-schema check fails, the SDK's own exception passes straight through. That's because you defined failure as the server marking the tool result as an error.\n- **A JSON `null` result is returned as text.** A tool whose structured output is literally `null` can't be told apart from one with no structured output, so `run_tool` returns its text instead."
  },
